<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Robert Phillips</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as James Phillips</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Robert Phillips was a British colonial official, acting consul-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Coast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>. In January 1897 he led a small, largely unarmed party towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a>, intending to see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>, despite warnings to wait until a sacred festival had ended. The party was ambushed and Phillips and most of his companions were killed, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> used as the pretext for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-expedition-of-1897">Benin Expedition of 1897</a>.</p></div>
